## 2. Useful Windows Network Commands

### `ipconfig`

Displays basic IP configuration.

```cmd
ipconfig
```

### `ipconfig /all`

Displays detailed network configuration, including IP address, subnet mask, default gateway, DHCP status, and DNS servers.

```cmd
ipconfig /all
```

### `ipconfig /release`

Releases the current DHCP-assigned IPv4 address.

```cmd
ipconfig /release
```

### `ipconfig /renew`

Requests a new DHCP lease.

```cmd
ipconfig /renew
```

Use these commands only when appropriate. Releasing an address can temporarily disconnect the device from the network.

### `ipconfig /flushdns`

Clears the local Windows DNS resolver cache.

```cmd
ipconfig /flushdns
```

This can help when a cached DNS result is causing a problem, but it does not repair every DNS issue.

### `ping`

Tests basic IP connectivity and response time.

```cmd
ping 192.168.1.1
ping 8.8.8.8
ping google.com
```

Note: A failed ping does not always mean a device or service is unavailable because firewalls may block ICMP traffic.

### `nslookup`

Queries DNS to check whether a hostname can be resolved.

```cmd
nslookup google.com
```

This is useful when investigating possible DNS problems.

